 1069 "q-36409" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124983 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124983 #edb/ref 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(-1,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124983 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5b10043b-af03-5e4b-8ef7-b7ee2892ed6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124983 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or inside the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/bb/bbc3e1bfb40c12cfdc655b4207c5f34ea0a01ce254b3f04b96b05ffbbad3f5af.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ and $Q(-1,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/10/100a2b2c1aa58cdfa59980cae4253e1de8b4b2c984c0793c66f7e7cc6edb5211.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ satisfy the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $Q$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is fully contained inside the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III.'" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125604 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125604 #edb/ref 1061 41386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125604 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "44c26f5a-57b1-5e3b-b126-e2f92e8f7bac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125604 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125605 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125605 #edb/ref 1061 41387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125605 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "13578b7b-3533-5f2d-b6bc-c8214f0f163b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125605 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125606 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125606 #edb/ref 1061 41433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125606 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b2201caf-3e1b-53b4-b391-8a27cfbbdce1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125606 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125607 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125607 #edb/ref 1061 41436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125607 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fd03cb93-6f1c-502a-9888-3a2700a9c8a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125607 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125615 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125615 #edb/ref 1061 41912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125615 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "23fe8ae7-b1b8-5490-93e8-49b20cd742f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125615 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125616 #edb/ref 1061 41916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125616 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dc8051c9-6e2f-5ded-82a6-e902adddd2c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125616 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125622 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125622 #edb/ref 1061 44624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125622 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "99fecfa4-9502-52e4-aff9-d84b074446bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125622 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125623 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125623 #edb/ref 1061 44625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125623 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2b346783-d47e-5992-9033-dcf4627c7e39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125623 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125863 #edb/ref 1064 "A **neighborhood** of a point $x_0\\in \\mathbb R$ is the set of points that are \"close to\" $x_0.$\n\nMore precisely, let's define a number $\\delta >0$, where $\\delta$ is usually very small. A **neighborhood** of a point $x_0$ is the set of all points whose distance from $x_0$ is smaller than $\\delta.$\n\nIn one-dimensional space, a neighborhood of a point $x_0\\in\\mathbb R$ is an open interval $\\left(x_0-\\delta, x_0+\\delta\\right),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/2e/2e28aded47a5f57c88b15a05ea032ec05527c0a47a6e5a72f029c5b5d6d9cc61.svg)\n\n![](images/04/04016c37be4c907eabc144db16eb757dff773907262ccd04941cf27ddd2e49d6.png)\n\n![](images/e9/e9455f39338547fa24cf28db5fbf3a925c8dedc6b6d9fe2d1411cc0139f9bdf5.svg)\n\nFor example, if we set $\\delta = 0.1,$ then the corresponding neighborhood of the point $x_0 = 2$ is as follows:\n\n![](images/08/08364d92317893fc21a0af75ccf3c824314e38edbb8a75e23b2206eebf191161.svg)\n\n![](images/03/0329bef56b8b903eb13510b7344fd7ca488d34c8d0c85c7648cebc8ecf7cfa40.png)\n\n![](images/f8/f8bddff4ee56ec85ef47a3f4404de1443a7d2c93491acb1933d97168d5092b7f.svg)\n\nNote that $x=1.9$ and $x=2.1$ are *not* included in this neighborhood since their distance from the point $x_0$ is *not* smaller than $\\delta.$\n\nWe can represent a neighborhood of $x_0$ using set notation as follows:\n\n$$\\{x : | x- x_0| < \\delta \\}$$\n\nWe often refer to sets of this type as *open* neighborhoods since they contain all points whose distance from $x_0$ is *smaller than* (but not equal) to $\\delta.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125863 #edb/ref 1065 6818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125863 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b2ea7b59-5287-5d12-b53f-32a9254b8ba7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125863 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125864 #edb/ref 1064 "In two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of a point $\\mathbf x_0\\in \\mathbb R^2$ is a disc of radius $\\delta$ centered at $\\mathbf x_0$ that does not include the bounding circle, as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nThe definitions of the interior and boundary points are the same as for the one-dimensional case. The only difference is that now a neighborhood of a point is a disc.\n\nFor example, consider the set $S$ and points $A,$ $B, C,$ and $D,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/a7/a7e037fd998c235ff7b721e7187d44814700ac5f41a256920f34f248c798c520.png)\n\nLet's classify each of these as interior or boundary points (or neither):\n\n- $A$ is an interior point since *there exists* a neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set.\n- $B$ is neither an interior nor a boundary point because *there exists* a neighborhood of $B$ that does not contain points from the set.\n- $C$ is a boundary point since *every* neighborhood of $C$ contains points that lie in the set and points that do not lie in the set.\n- $D$ is a boundary point since *every* neighborhood of $D$ contains points that lie in the set and points that do not lie in the set.\n\nNotice that although $C$ and $D$ are both boundary points, $C\\notin S$ and yet $D\\in S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125864 #edb/ref 1065 6825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125864 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bfda97a2-2fe7-5c9a-a067-dbf903ba2111" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125864 #edb/ref 1067 "Interior and Boundary Points of Sets in the Plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125870 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, suppose that $S$ is a set of real numbers. A point $x_0$ is called an **interior point** of $S$ if *some* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ is *fully contained* inside $S.$\n\nFor example, consider the set $S=[0,1)$ and the points $A$ and $B,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/22/22a3429a80227c296fb911ce0caefd282e4066c70da10cf9fe9740a5523a0da7.svg)\n\n![](images/a1/a12c60b6f60ca7c35162fb1ddf0d0d507fe80e73e621c451c21f0b882034ae21.png)\n\n![](images/2e/2e28aded47a5f57c88b15a05ea032ec05527c0a47a6e5a72f029c5b5d6d9cc61.svg)\n\nThe point $A$ is an interior point of $S,$ but $B$ is not:\n\n- The point $A$ is an interior point of $S$ since there exists a neighborhood of $A$ that is *fully contained* inside $S.$\n- The point $B$ is *not* an interior point of $S$ since there is no neighborhood of $B$ that is *fully contained* inside $S.$ In other words, any neighborhood of $B$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nNote that neither $x=0$ nor $x=1$ are interior points. To see why, let's draw a neighborhood of each point:\n\n![](images/f3/f320e2e063366c71293fb0d5433ccd75ef5794dce9c69f9780c52610823b13a8.svg)\n\n![](images/a9/a923732241443a0fd6a5f53dab7de2d346f5704a0b034c276e64dc3df2746f4f.png)\n\nFrom here, we can deduce the following:\n\n- The point $x=0$ is *not* an interior point of $S$ since there is no neighborhood of $x=0$ that is *fully* contained inside $S.$\n- The point $x=1$ is also *not* an interior point of $S$ since there is no neighborhood of $x=1$ that is *fully* contained inside $S.$\n\nThe set containing all interior points of a set $S$ is called the **interior** of $S,$ and is denoted $\\textrm{int}\\,S.$\n\nFor the set $[0,1),$ its interior is given by\n\n$$\\textrm{int}\\,S = (0,1).$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125870 #edb/ref 1065 7685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125870 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "da6d3c21-fe0a-566e-a6e0-117f10988923" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125870 #edb/ref 1067 "Interior Points" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125871 #edb/ref 1064 "A point $x_0$ is a **boundary point** of $S$ if *every* neighborhood of $x_0$ contains points that *are* in $S$ *and* points that are *not* in $S.$\n\nLet's consider the set $S=[0,1),$ the points $x=0, x=1,$ and neighborhoods of these points.\n\n![](images/81/81d196b07cb70c7440bbf32cd23c2929b0e10f883f5bfdc90b087830dab5fdee.svg)\n\n![](images/a9/a923732241443a0fd6a5f53dab7de2d346f5704a0b034c276e64dc3df2746f4f.png)\n\n![](images/22/22a3429a80227c296fb911ce0caefd282e4066c70da10cf9fe9740a5523a0da7.svg)\n\nBoth $x=0$ and $x=1$ are boundary points of $S{:}$\n\n- The point $x=0$ is a boundary point because *every* open neighborhood contains points that belong to $S$ and points that do not belong to $S.$\n- The point $x=1$ is a boundary point because *every* open neighborhood contains points that belong to $S$ and points that do not belong to $S.$\n\nThe set of all boundary points of a set $S$ is called the **boundary** of $S,$ and is denoted as $\\partial S.$\n\nFor the set $[0,1)$ above, its boundary contains two points only, namely $x=0$ and $x=1,$ and we write\n\n$$\\partial S = \\{0,1\\}$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125871 #edb/ref 1065 7686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125871 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "54b0ce40-c312-54e6-87c5-ba2b69af3dae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125871 #edb/ref 1067 "Boundary Points" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417784 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186417819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417784 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186417820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417784 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186417821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417784 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186417822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417784 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186417823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417784 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417784 #edb/ref 1045 "Interior and Boundary Points" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417784 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186417821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417784 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417784 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417784 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "1525b4dc-2341-50b4-9999-2ad38292c9ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417785 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417785 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417785 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0972b4fe-a6de-5b28-91e1-b8f15d278acf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417786 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417786 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417786 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "16604d5a-129d-54c3-83f1-2dbdb9c13b77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417787 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417787 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417787 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "18dc53b3-03ab-5649-8fb0-de461024a5e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417788 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417788 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417788 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1b2bf559-c6e4-56a2-a728-3d0c5adf819f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417789 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417789 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417789 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "26c9aa3e-4575-55fe-bef6-0e504f1fb60e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] 