[17592186417811 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186417806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417811 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417811 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417811 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186417802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417811 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7e293cef-2f84-5bc4-9212-2735610d6da3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186417787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186417788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186417791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186417800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186417801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417812 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417812 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417812 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186417788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417812 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a5bc6201-b1a5-5eb7-b325-cd8c3c42ef38" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417813 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186417785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417813 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186417789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417813 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186417792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417813 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186417796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417813 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186417803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417813 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417813 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417813 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186417789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417813 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b71b8e04-6ae9-5c68-998d-3aaf22c66038" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417814 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186417786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417814 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186417793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417814 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186417794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417814 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186417797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417814 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186417809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417814 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417814 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417814 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186417793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417814 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e9aa40d2-6ab6-5496-ab09-1db4a0c06d75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417815 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417815 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71826" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417815 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417815 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b8/b838b945d0b4002d8ae2238af5992c83dcf23b2985c448c716a5caf8de31b369.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417815 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4db015a0-682b-5ff4-909b-ad19f05ab168" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417815 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/be/be1ece00243777f3f6b6973319e15ea1e5c8556b2452b61a451713f9dcb86595.png)\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only'." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417816 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417816 #edb/ref 1069 "q-158601" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417816 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417816 #edb/ref 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y<-1},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=-1}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(1,-1)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(1,-1)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(2,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417816 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7e4c0564-b5b1-55bc-bc17-3dffa3a951a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417816 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie below the line $y=-1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/75/75d5d199d12cc2f3170822a40c52762fcc8368caac26a8134661f9ac72ab462b.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,-1)$ and $Q(2,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/81/818da293be040897d3a536b396d6fe7ff992f3d91f976301e14a6253356b5bde.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(1,-1)$ satisfy $y=-1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(-1,1)$ do not satisfy the inequality $y<-1.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417817 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417817 #edb/ref 1069 "q-158547" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417817 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417817 #edb/ref 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y>2x-3},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=2x-3}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(1,-1)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $Q(0,0)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(0,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417817 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dbe53a96-4c4c-5897-ba48-d4861ec6cb49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417817 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie above the line $y=2x-3.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/5a/5af40a400e5ea4942a1cf89b09184a277df96d252e4a7fee41bd2df7024a631f.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,-1)$ and $Q(0,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/27/27b978864eac65c93c7886b2d54dfede7130f0f93a16ef96421cd0e5741a6940.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(1,-1)$ satisfy $y=2x-3.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the coordinates of $Q(0,0)$ satisfy the inequality $y>2x-3.$ Hence, $Q$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is entirely contained inside the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III.'" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417818 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417818 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159495" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417818 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417